  <h1>Streaming Apps: What Netflix and Spotify Collect and Share About You</h1>
  <div class="byline">Scan My Shadow &middot; September 2, 2026</div>

  <div class="tldr">
    <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Streaming platforms collect far more than viewing or listening history — device information, precise usage timing, payment details, and behavioral patterns that feed into recommendation and, in some cases, advertising systems. Here's a realistic look at what's gathered and what you can actually control.</p>
  </div>


  <p>It's easy to think of a streaming subscription as a simple transaction — you pay, you watch or listen. In practice, these platforms run on detailed behavioral data, and understanding what's collected clarifies both how recommendations work and what's shared beyond the platform itself.</p>

  <h2>What streaming platforms typically collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Detailed viewing or listening behavior</strong> — not just what you watched, but when you paused, rewound, skipped, or abandoned content partway through.</li>
    <li><strong>Device and network information</strong> — what devices you use, roughly where you're connecting from, and how many concurrent streams are active.</li>
    <li><strong>Payment and billing details</strong> — held by the platform or its payment processor, depending on the service.</li>
    <li><strong>Cross-referencing with profiles</strong> — on shared or family accounts, activity is often tracked per profile, building individual behavioral patterns even within one household subscription.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this data goes beyond the app itself</h2>
  <p>Some streaming platforms partner with advertising and analytics companies, particularly on ad-supported tiers, which have become more common in India's streaming market as platforms diversify pricing. Even on ad-free tiers, behavioral data is often used for internal recommendation algorithms and occasionally shared in aggregated or anonymized form with content partners for licensing and production decisions.</p>

  <h2>What you can actually do about it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Review each platform's privacy settings for data-sharing and personalized-advertising toggles, which exist on most major services even if not prominently placed.</li>
    <li>Use individual profiles rather than sharing a single login across a household, both for account security and to keep behavioral profiles more accurately separated.</li>
    <li>Periodically request or review your data export where the platform offers one — most major streaming services provide this under account settings.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Streaming activity is a relatively low-stakes category of exposure compared to breach data or leaked identity documents, but it's still part of the broader picture of what's tracked about you across the apps you use daily. For the higher-stakes side — what's exposed tied to your phone number and email specifically — a <a href="https://www.scanmyshadow.com">Scan My Shadow</a> report is the more relevant check.</p>


  <div class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <h3>Do streaming platforms sell my viewing data to advertisers?</h3>
    <p>Practices vary by platform and by pricing tier — ad-supported tiers are more likely to share data with advertising partners, while premium ad-free tiers typically limit this, though internal recommendation use still applies broadly.</p>
    <h3>Can I see what data a streaming service has collected about me?</h3>
    <p>Most major platforms offer a data export or activity history option under account settings, though the level of detail shown varies.</p>
    <h3>Is my payment information exposed to the streaming platform itself?</h3>
    <p>It's typically handled through the platform's payment processor, with the platform itself usually storing limited billing details rather than full card information.</p>
    <h3>Does sharing an account with family affect my privacy?</h3>
    <p>Yes — unless separate profiles are used, viewing behavior can blend together, and if profiles aren't correctly separated, individual habits may be less clearly attributed.</p>
    <h3>Is this kind of data collection a serious privacy risk?</h3>
    <p>It's generally lower-stakes than breach or identity data, but it's still worth understanding as part of your overall digital footprint across the apps you use regularly.</p>
  </div>

